{"causes":["The rise of Gnosticism in the 1st century CE, which sought to reconcile Christian teachings with ancient Greek and Jewish philosophical traditions.","The discovery of the Nag Hammadi library in Egypt in 1945, which provided a treasure trove of Gnostic texts including the Gospel of Philip.","The rediscovery of the Coptic language and script, which allowed scholars to better understand the text's contents and historical context."],"effects":["The spread of Gnosticism throughout the Mediterranean world, particularly among urban populations and intellectual circles.","The development of Western esotericism as a distinct philosophical and spiritual tradition, drawing on Gnostic ideas and symbolism.","The ongoing fascination with Gnosticism among scholars, theologians, and enthusiasts, which has led to increased interest in ancient texts like the Gospel of Philip."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"nag-hammadi-library","sourceName":"Nag Hammadi Library","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Nag Hammadi library is a collection of Gnostic texts, including the Gospel of Philip."},{"sourceSlug":"coptic-language","sourceName":"Coptic Language","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Coptic language played a crucial role in the composition and transmission of the Gospel of Philip."},{"sourceSlug":"christianity","sourceName":"Christianity","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Gospel of Philip's ideas reflect a synthesis of Christian and Gnostic teachings."},{"sourceSlug":"western-esotericism","sourceName":"Western Esotericism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Gospel of Philip's ideas have had a lasting impact on Western esotericism."},{"sourceSlug":"greece","sourceName":"Ancient Greece","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Gospel of Philip's Coptic text reflects the influence of Greek philosophical traditions."},{"sourceSlug":"judaism","sourceName":"Judaism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Gospel of Philip's ideas reflect a synthesis of Jewish and Gnostic teachings."},{"sourceSlug":"frithjof-schuon","sourceName":"Frithjof Schuon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"Schuon drew upon the Gospel of Philip in his own esoteric writings."},{"sourceSlug":"rene-guenon","sourceName":"René Guénon","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"Guénon's work often engaged with the ideas and symbolism presented in the Gospel of Philip."},{"sourceSlug":"nag-hammadi-library-discovery","sourceName":"Nag Hammadi Library Discovery","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Nag Hammadi library was rediscovered in 1945, leading to the reevaluation of Gnostic texts like the Gospel of Philip."},{"sourceSlug":"christianity-tradition","sourceName":"Christianity Tradition","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"gospel-of-philip","targetName":"Gospel of Philip","context":"The Gospel of Philip is part of the larger Christian tradition, reflecting its relationship to other Gnostic and orthodox texts."}],"places":[{"name":"Nag Hammadi","role":"Discovery Site"},{"name":"Egypt","role":"Country of Origin"},{"name":"Alexandria","role":"City of Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Gospel of Philip","type":"Reference article","slug":"gospel-of-philip"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q60867","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gospel_of_Philip"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"Gospel of Philip contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Gospel of Philip is a Gnostic text discovered at Nag Hammadi in Egypt in 1945 — Codex II, tractate 3 of the Nag Hammadi library — and dated by scholars to the 3rd century CE, probably composed in the Syrian Christian tradition and reflecting the theology of the Valentinian Gnostic school. Unlike the Gospel of Thomas (a collection of sayings) or the Gospel of Mary (a dialogue), the Gospel of Philip is an anthology of theological reflections, spiritual aphorisms, and commentary on the Christian sacraments — covering baptism, chrism (anointing), the Eucharist, redemption, and the bridal chamber — with particular emphasis on the spiritual significance of sacramental union and the concept of the 'bridal chamber' as the consummation of spiritual knowledge (gnosis). The text contains approximately 100 pericopes of varying length in Coptic translation from a Greek original.

The Gospel of Philip is particularly known for its references to Mary Magdalene as Jesus's 'companion' (koinônos) and the statement that Jesus 'loved her more than [all] the disciples and used to kiss her on her [mouth — the lacuna in the manuscript leaves the body part uncertain]' — a passage that has been widely interpreted (and misinterpreted, particularly in popular culture influenced by The Da Vinci Code) as suggesting a romantic or marital relationship between Jesus and Mary Magdalene, but which in its Valentinian theological context more plausibly reflects the spiritual symbolism of the bridal chamber gnosis. The Gospel of Philip's Mary Magdalene passages, read alongside the Gospel of Mary, provided the popular foundation for feminist theological arguments about Mary Magdalene's spiritual authority.

The Valentinian theological framework of the Gospel of Philip — its emphasis on sacramental knowledge, the spiritual significance of the bridal chamber, the distinction between psychic and pneumatic (spiritual) Christians, and its complex treatment of truth, falsehood, and the nature of names — reflects the sophisticated sacramental theology of one of the major Gnostic schools of the 2nd–3rd centuries CE. Its discovery at Nag Hammadi contributed substantially to the modern scholarly understanding of Valentinian Gnosticism.","subjects":["Gnostic Christianity","Classical Era","Nag Hammadi","Valentinian Gnosticism","Mary Magdalene","Early Christianity","Sacramental Theology","Biblical Scholarship"],"frameworks":["RELIGIOUS_INTERPRETATION","STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Gospel of Philip is a significant text within the broader context of Gnosticism and Christian thought, offering insights into the spiritual practices and beliefs of early Christians.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
